{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 2909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Novium for supporting PBS"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2909,
    "dDurationMs": 4768,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re almost certainly the first technological\ncivilization on Earth. But what if we’re"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7677,
    "dDurationMs": 7074,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not? No. We are. But how sure are we, really?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20020,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A favorite way to estimate the abundance of\nlife in the universe is the famous Drake equation,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25090,
    "dDurationMs": 4301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which figures that the number of intelligent\nalien civilizations in our own galaxy who"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29391,
    "dDurationMs": 4799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might overlap with us can be estimated by\nmultiplying a chain of factors, basically"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34190,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "summarized as the number of habitable planets\ntimes the probability of a planet forming"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38960,
    "dDurationMs": 5424,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life, then technological life, then factoring\nin how long that civilization survives."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44384,
    "dDurationMs": 4366,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We now know that there are billions of habitable\nplanets in our galaxy, but the other parameters"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48750,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are still extremely difficult to guess. And\nthat’s because we only have one example"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52930,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the formation of life and civilization\nto go off. And we have zero examples of a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57620,
    "dDurationMs": 5369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technological species going extinct, although\nwe’re on track to get at least one of those."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62989,
    "dDurationMs": 4971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we detect just one more instance of life\nor technology out there we’ll immediately"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67960,
    "dDurationMs": 5199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be able to upgrade our guess at the probability\nof any given planet reaching that stage of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73159,
    "dDurationMs": 6791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "development. But you know what would increase\nthe probability even more? If we saw it happen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79950,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "twice on the same planet. There’s an extremely\nloose piece of evidence, not even evidence,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86470,
    "dDurationMs": 5579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maybe just “teaser” that life may have\nstarted independently twice on earth. There’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92049,
    "dDurationMs": 6061,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this zircon crystal found in the west of Australia\nthat contains a tiny speck of carbon that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98110,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looks like it was filtered through a living\nmetabolism. It has the characteristic higher"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103700,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proportion of carbon-12 versus carbon-13 that\nplants breath out. That speck of “biogenic”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109600,
    "dDurationMs": 7729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "carbon was probably been perfectly sealed\nin a zircon crystal probably for around 4.1"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117329,
    "dDurationMs": 5801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "billion years old, dating it to before Earth’s\ncrust was probably re-liquified by massive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123130,
    "dDurationMs": 3330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "asteroid bombardment that should have extinguished\nall life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126460,
    "dDurationMs": 7139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s a lot of probabilities, but this\nis crazy given that the next earliest fossils–"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133599,
    "dDurationMs": 6041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in Australia, and also in Greenland–date\nto between 3.5 and 3.8 billion years ago after"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139640,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Earth re-solidified following its pounding.\nIf so then abiogenesis–life arising from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "non-life–would have to have happened again.\nAnd that’d mean that life really does start"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151640,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very quickly given the right conditions. We’d\nhave to update our Drake equation estimate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157110,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the probability of life forming given a\nsuitable planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160550,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But all this is extremely speculative–and\none reason is that it’s really hard to verify"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165120,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the origin of life is because these fossils\nare extremely scarce. Greenland and that bit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171640,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Western Australia are the only fragments\nof land still left on the surface from that time. The entirety"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177420,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the rest of the crust has been subducted–pulled\nback into Earth’s mantle by tectonic activity–not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183890,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "once but many times. The crust is recycled\nroughly every half billion years, meaning"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189250,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much of the early fossil record has been deleted,\nwhich makes it extraordinarily difficult to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194080,
    "dDurationMs": 2030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "track the early rise of life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196110,
    "dDurationMs": 4911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if the geological record is so crappy,\nthen in half a billion years Earth’s new"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201021,
    "dDurationMs": 6729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inhabitants will have precious little evidence\nthat we ever existed. So could we even know"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207750,
    "dDurationMs": 7590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we are the first technological civilization on Earth? And if\nwe’re not the first, that would also have huge implications"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215340,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for our Drake equation, forcing us to update\nboth the probabilities of technological civilizations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220310,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arising from life, and also our lifespan estimates\nfor those civilizations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226280,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I know this sounds like some lizard-illuminati rubbish, but\none effort to properly address an out-there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231840,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proposal like this is a paper by NASA climatologist\nGavin Schmidt and physicist and astronomer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237920,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Adam Frank from a few years ago. They propose\nthe Silurian hypothesis, which asks whether"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243670,
    "dDurationMs": 6330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pre-human industrial civilizations might have\nexisted. The name is an old-school Doctor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250000,
    "dDurationMs": 4269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Who reference. I’ll leave you to google\nthat to see what we’re actually looking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254269,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255269,
    "dDurationMs": 5051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let me be quite serious for a moment: I don’t\nthink such a civilization existed. Schmidt"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260320,
    "dDurationMs": 5129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Frank don’t think such a civilization\nexisted. The burden of proof of such a claim"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265449,
    "dDurationMs": 6451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is massive, and massively on the claimant,\nand currently there is no real evidence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271900,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The actual purpose of this video and of the\nSilurian hypothesis is to frame a very clear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277330,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scientific question around a very speculative idea. So let’s do just that. Broadly that question"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283710,
    "dDurationMs": 6650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be: Could a non-human industrial civilization\nhave existed before us even given our current"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290360,
    "dDurationMs": 7290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lack of evidence of such? And if so, how long\nago would it have had to have existed in order"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297650,
    "dDurationMs": 6739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be completely erased by now? And finally\nwhat traces of such might still exist that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304389,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we could now look for?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305389,
    "dDurationMs": 4601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But first, to start thinking about what evidence\na past civilization may have left behind,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309990,
    "dDurationMs": 4209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let’s think about what our civilization\nwill look like in the far future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314199,
    "dDurationMs": 6131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our species has been around for 300-ish thousand\nyears, but has only had a major impact for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320330,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the past 10-ish thousand years of the Anthropocene\nera–by definition–it’s named as the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326860,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "era of our major impact. And that impact has\nmassively accelerated in the 300-ish years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333500,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "post-industrial revolution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335220,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When you look at the world now, you might\nthink that our constructions are the most"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340990,
    "dDurationMs": 4959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "telling signs of our presence once we’re\ngone. But that won’t be the case for long."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345949,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The pyramids may have lasted millenia, but\nthey won’t last millions of years. All of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350639,
    "dDurationMs": 7301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our great cities will erode to dust, be covered\nup or washed away or end up as deserts, then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357940,
    "dDurationMs": 4971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ocean floors then deep sedimentary layers\nthen mountains then deserts again. Much will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362911,
    "dDurationMs": 7759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also be pulled beneath shifting tectonic plates–subducted.\nIt’ll be reforged into new rock in the mantle–its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370670,
    "dDurationMs": 6730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geological memory almost completely wiped\nbefore it reemerges, if it ever does. So will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377400,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any sign of these works be apparent to a far future?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381420,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The recycling of the mantle means that anything\nolder than half a billion years is pretty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385870,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much gone. But we do find plenty of fossilized\ndinosaur bones of dinosaurs that are 100s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391950,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of millions of years old. So surely we’d\nfind remnants of a civilization that’s only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396500,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "millions of years old. But that is not necessarily the case, because of the potential limited"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402510,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extent of the civilization, both spatially\nand temporally."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407700,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have of course obtained samples of the\nEarth’s surface from many millions of years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411740,
    "dDurationMs": 6850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ago. Some of those areas are still exposed,\nbut most we reach by digging or drilling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418590,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But here’s the thing, we’ve accessed only\na tiny fraction of a percent of the original"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424870,
    "dDurationMs": 6519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth surface from before the Quaternary period,\n2.6 million years ago. And modern urban land"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431389,
    "dDurationMs": 5851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coverage is less than 1% of Earth’s surface\ntoday. So, if a civilization just like us"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437240,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "existed a few million years ago, it’s extremely\nunlikely that we, today, would have stumbled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442650,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on their physical remnants. That includes\ncities and artifacts, but also fossils. The"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448670,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fossilization rate is so low that we’ve\nonly discovered a small fraction of the species"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454120,
    "dDurationMs": 2359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that ever existed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456479,
    "dDurationMs": 6761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember that the dinosaurs roamed the Earth\nfrom around 240 to 65 million years ago. They’ve"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463240,
    "dDurationMs": 6109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been gone for a third of the time they were\naround. And across those 180 million years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469349,
    "dDurationMs": 6441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we have just a handful of specimens. By comparison,\nour 10,000 years of civilization is barely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475790,
    "dDurationMs": 6790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a blip in the fossil record, our industrial\nera not even a blip. We can find T-rexes,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482580,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but we can’t find T-rexes from a particular\nfew centuries."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486530,
    "dDurationMs": 5949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ll elaborate on the extent of the Anthropocene\nblip: our best way to trace geological time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492479,
    "dDurationMs": 5361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is in sedimentary rock. This is rock that\noriginally formed from the precipitation of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497840,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dead ocean things onto the ocean floor, building\nup over millions of years. From sedimentary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502900,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rock that has since been exposed, or from\ndeep cores drilled from the oceans, we can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508030,
    "dDurationMs": 5309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "analyze these layers to track changes in ocean\nchemistry, temperature, and biological content"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513339,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from these layers, which in turn reflect those\nsame properties on a global scale."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518099,
    "dDurationMs": 6191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The entire Anthropocene will be represented\nby a layer only several centimeters thick"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524290,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in kilometers of sedimentary layers, with\nthe industrial age a proportionally tiny fraction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529820,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of that–perhaps millimeters at best."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533140,
    "dDurationMs": 4221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although a far-future civilization probably\nwouldn't find our bones or artifacts, they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537361,
    "dDurationMs": 6589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might find and recognize this wafer thin layer\nin the geological record. As our structures"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543950,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and cities grew, so did our broader footprint.\n“Anthropocene” is the word for the geological"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550310,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "era dominated by human activity. It’s not\nquite textbook yet, but probably will be soon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556630,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Geological eras are periods of time reflected\nby distinct changes in the geological record."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562160,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Anthropocene certainly qualifies. In fact,\nthe geological marker currently being laid"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568060,
    "dDurationMs": 6050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down in new sedimentary layers due to our\nactivities bears an eerie resemblance to some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574110,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the transitions in the geological record\nof millions of years ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577800,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So to assess whether any of those previous\nevents might be connected to industrial activity,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584560,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let’s take a look at the specifics of how\nwe’re going to confuse future geologists"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589480,
    "dDurationMs": 4539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and paleontologists with the crap we’re\nsprinkling over the surface of the Earth that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594019,
    "dDurationMs": 5061,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are already seen in the newly-laid sedimentary\nlayers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599080,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First there are the more direct chemical and\nisotopic imbalances due to us making a bunch"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604300,
    "dDurationMs": 4909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of weird stuff. Industrial pollutants like\nheavy metals and chemicals such as CFCs and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609209,
    "dDurationMs": 6581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their long-lived byproducts. Consumer waste\nlike rare earth elements and plastics. Nitrogenous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615790,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fertilizers and even steroids from large-scale\nfarming. Radioactive isotopes deposited globally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621380,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from nuclear weapons testing that will last\ntens of millions of years. Then there’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625890,
    "dDurationMs": 5259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the effect of habitat destruction and species\nextinction, which won’t be so much a distinct"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631149,
    "dDurationMs": 5171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layer in the geological record as a sudden\ndrop in the markers of biodiversity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636320,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But our main geological impact is, of course,\nclimate change. Remember that speck of biogenic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642510,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "carbon in the Aussie crystal? This is more\nthan a spec. Over the industrial revolution,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648380,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around half a trillion tons of carbon that’s\nrich in the C-12 isotope has been pumped into the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654390,
    "dDurationMs": 6230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "air. This is increasing the ratio of C-12\nto C-13 in the atmosphere, which is then reflected"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660620,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the soil and oceans, and ultimately in\nthe sedimentary layers currently being built."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665760,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The rise in temperature associated with this new atmospheric carbon will influence the geological record in a number of ways."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672920,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Increased rainfall and rising sea levels increase\nerosion even further. Warmer oceans give up"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677820,
    "dDurationMs": 4949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more of their dissolved CO2, increasing their\nacidity; this and the temperature change itself"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682769,
    "dDurationMs": 7601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alters the fauna that can live in it and ultimately\nlayer on the ocean floor. There are many other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690370,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "known and unknown effects beyond these."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693990,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So from all this it sounds like it’ll be\neasy for a future civilization to see where"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697940,
    "dDurationMs": 6570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we scrawled “we was here” in the geological\nrecord. And if so, we should be able to find"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704510,
    "dDurationMs": 7350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a prior one. Except that so many of these signals can be duplicated by natural phenomena. We’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711860,
    "dDurationMs": 4219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "come back to those natural explanations, but\nfirst let’s see if there are any interesting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716079,
    "dDurationMs": 5841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "past geological markers that look anything\nat all like the one we’re currently forging."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721920,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s go hunting for pre-human technological\ncivilizations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726110,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our geological record contains several candidate\n“weird layers” that also represent massive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732000,
    "dDurationMs": 6820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "environmental shifts. Some we understand well–like\nthe Cretaceous–Paleogene boundary, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738820,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is connected to a dinosaur-hating asteroid\nhitting the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743340,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But some still don’t have broadly accepted\nexplanations. And here we get to the Silurian"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748950,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hypothesis, which asks if any of these as-yet-unexplained\nabrupt shifts in the geological record could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755870,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have been due to an industrial civilization."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758990,
    "dDurationMs": 7710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Schmidt and Frank focus on two broad types,\nwhich have a lot overlap. We have hyperthermals–largely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766700,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the Eocene from 56 to 34 million years\nago. These are characterized by rapid increases"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774380,
    "dDurationMs": 7370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in global temperatures and are often accompanied\nby significant shifts in carbon isotope ratios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781750,
    "dDurationMs": 5519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The latter suggest that the temperature shift\nis due to a rapid injection of CO2 from burning"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787269,
    "dDurationMs": 6721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of some organic fuel. We are triggering a\nhyperthermal now, but could some of the past"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793990,
    "dDurationMs": 6770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hyperthermals also have been triggered by\nsomething like us? Not impossible, but stand-by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800760,
    "dDurationMs": 2718,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the debunkings."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803478,
    "dDurationMs": 6221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then we have ocean anoxic events, a few of\nwhich are found in the earlier Cretaceous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809699,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Jurassic periods. These are characterized\nby a rapid drop in the oxygenation of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815639,
    "dDurationMs": 6441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oceans, accompanied by a great dying of ocean\nlife. We’re seeing the beginning of a potential"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822080,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“OAE” right now. As in our case, the ancient\nOAEs are often accompanied by shifts in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830120,
    "dDurationMs": 7209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CO2 isotopic ratios, and so could also have\nbeen triggered by climate change."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837329,
    "dDurationMs": 5371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Going back further, there have been several\nabrupt shifts since around 500 million years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842700,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ago involving combinations of the signals\nI’ve already described. And the further"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847680,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back we go the harder it is to pin down the\ndefinite cause, so there’s more and more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852820,
    "dDurationMs": 6050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "room for our flights of fancy about dinosaur\nempires."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858870,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The biggest challenge to assessing the Silurian\nhypothesis is that natural climate shifts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863860,
    "dDurationMs": 6590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can duplicate so many of the current anthropogenic\nsignals–from the changes in temperature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870450,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the carbon situation to the different\ntypes of minerals being deposited on the ocean"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876000,
    "dDurationMs": 5639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "floor due to changes in ocean life. And there\nare many potential natural causes for climate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881639,
    "dDurationMs": 5661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "change–for example, the periodic shifts\nin Earth’s orbit characterized by the Milankovic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887300,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cycles, which we covered once upon a time,\nand which are correlated with past climate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891880,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shifts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892880,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if a signal is not caused by the climate\nchange itself, then it might be share a cause"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898430,
    "dDurationMs": 6899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with that climate change. Spikes in heavy\nmetals or rare earth elements can come from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905329,
    "dDurationMs": 5371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technology manufacturing, or be produced by\nvolcanic eruptions which spew vast quantities"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910700,
    "dDurationMs": 5499,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of minerals and metals into the atmosphere\nand oceans. Radioactive isotopes could be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916199,
    "dDurationMs": 7921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to a nuclear program or due to a nearby\nsupernova explosion. Layers of soot and particulates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924120,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in sediment could indicate combustion processes\nfrom an ancient industry, or come from widespread"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930630,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wildfires or an asteroid impact. These can\nsend soot and dust into the upper atmosphere,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936510,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eventually settling around the globe. And\nmore importantly, can trigger a shift in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941690,
    "dDurationMs": 6709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "climate if enough CO2 is released. There’s\ngood evidence that the Paleocene-Eocene Thermal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948399,
    "dDurationMs": 5941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maximum–the first and greatest hyperthermal\nof the Eocene, was caused by a giant magma"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954340,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "zone intruding on a giant fossil fuel bed.\nAnd these sorts of catastrophes and the associated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961420,
    "dDurationMs": 5589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "climate change can also trigger the type of\nmass extinction that we expect to accompany"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967009,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our own geological book-end."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969709,
    "dDurationMs": 6171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That said, there are some things we’re doing\nto our own geological layer that would be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975880,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hard to explain naturally. Some of the long-lived\nsynthetic chemicals really have no known natural"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982170,
    "dDurationMs": 6310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "source, like some of these industrial fluorides.\nThere are things like the chirality of molecules–the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988480,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "left- or right-handedness of their symmetry–that\nis strongly one way in nature, but random"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994170,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in our industrial production."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996370,
    "dDurationMs": 6969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I should also point out that past sudden climate\nshifts appear to have been significantly less"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003339,
    "dDurationMs": 5101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sudden than our own. So if we found a shift\nthat occurred on the timescale of centuries"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008440,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than 10s of thousands of years, that\nmight be an indication. But it’s extremely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011660,
    "dDurationMs": 3099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "difficult to get that sort of time resolution\nin the geological record. In general this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014759,
    "dDurationMs": 5411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "points to a bit of a paradox: a civilization\nwhich perished due to climate change would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020170,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leave such a narrow record it might be invisible.\nOn the other hand, a much longer lived and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1026370,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so presumably environmentally sustainable\nadvanced civilization might produce minimal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031530,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ecological disruption over millenia, and so\nwould similarly be almost invisible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037230,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In any case, distinguishing between these\nartificial and natural scenarios requires"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042980,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extremely careful analysis of the context,\ndistribution, and composition of these markers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048980,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and their association with each other, all\nwhile piecing together clues that could span"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053520,
    "dDurationMs": 1570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geological epochs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055090,
    "dDurationMs": 7100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So have we detected signs of industry in ancient geological transitions? Nope. But our search"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1062190,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has also been limited. And this is the true\nvalue of the Silurian hypothesis, as Schmidt"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067440,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Frank themselves emphasize. It’s not\nto propose pre-human industrial civilizations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073140,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a likely explanation for past geological\nevents, rather its to refine our understanding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078190,
    "dDurationMs": 3590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of what to look for if we wanted to find evidence\nof such."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081780,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And although it seems extraordinarily unlikely,\nthe implications of discovering such a pre-human"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087460,
    "dDurationMs": 5959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "civilization would be impossible to overstate.\nBoth for our sense of our own place on this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1093419,
    "dDurationMs": 6111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planet, for our understanding of the likely\nabundance of civilization in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1099530,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It can even guide us in looking for past life–even\npast civilizations on other worlds, starting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1105010,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with Mars. But most importantly, discovering\nan extinct people who reached the same or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1110450,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "higher level of advancement as ourselves would\ngive us a stunning new perspective on our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1116130,
    "dDurationMs": 6490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "own future and a profound reminder of our\nfragility. Perhaps enough of a reminder to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1122620,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "allow us to avoid that hypothetical predecessor’s\nfate, and avoid becoming just another local"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1128050,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geological blip that had once dreamed of exploring\nall of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1133730,
    "dDurationMs": 7010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Novium for supporting PBS. Inspired\nby space, the Novium Hoverpen 2.0: Interstellar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1140740,
    "dDurationMs": 6650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Edition is a high-end pen designed to defy\ngravity at 23.5 degree angle to be a subtle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1147390,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reminder of our home planet’s axial tilt.\nWhether writing physics formulas or taking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153020,
    "dDurationMs": 4889,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "notes on your favorite show, the Hoverpen,\nis available in Starlight Silver, Mars Magma"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1157909,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Neptune Blue. But if you choose Space\nBlack, you can also own a hoverpen that contains"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1164059,
    "dDurationMs": 5081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a piece of the Muonionalusta meteorite - which\nlanded in northern Scandinavia more than a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1169140,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "million years ago. Each meteorite fragment\nis accompanied by a stainless steel certificate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1175080,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "certifying its origin. Novium offers worldwide\nshipping, and gift options are available."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1181330,
    "dDurationMs": 9880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's a link in the description to learn\nmore."
    } ]
  } ]
}
